==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|certeynte||surety}}, from {{der|en|xno|certeinte}}, from {{der|en|fro|certeinete}}, from {{der|en|VL.|*certānitās}}, from {{der|en|la|certus}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsɝtn̩ti/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsɜːtn̩ti/}}
* {{hyphenation|en|cer|tain|ty}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-certainty.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The state of being [[certain]].
#: {{syn|en|certitude|sureness}}
#: {{ant|en|doubt|uncertainty}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|October 12, 1786|{{w|Fisher Ames}}|Lucius Junius Brutus|Independent Chronicle|text=The '''certainty''' of punishment is the truest security against crimes.}}
# An instance of being certain.
# A fact or truth unquestionably established.
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:sure thing}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|November 2 2014|Daniel Taylor|url=http://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/nov/02/manchester-city-manchester-united-premier-league-match-report|Sergio Agüero strike wins derby for Manchester City against 10-man United|guardian.co.uk|text=Yet the truth is that City would probably have been coasting by that point if the referee, Michael Oliver, had not turned down three separate penalties, at least two of which could be accurately described as '''certainties'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Landor Imaginary Conversations|volume=1|text='''Certainties''' are uninteresting and sating.}}

